Assume AC for the library's standard-normal-law convention. Let a rooted tree have root $o$, one shared link $e_0=(o,v)$, and two branch links $e_1=(v,r_1)$ and $e_2=(v,r_2)$ to leaf receivers. On each probe, the three link delays are independent with laws $X_i\sim N(\mu_i,1)$, $i=0,1,2$, and the observed path delays are $Y_1=X_0+X_1$ and $Y_2=X_0+X_2$. The distinct link-mean vectors $\mu=(0,0,0)$ and $\mu'=(1,-1,-1)$ induce the same joint law of $(Y_1,Y_2)$; hence the three link means are not identifiable. The same equality holds for any number of independent repeated probes with these fixed link laws.

The diagram labels the shared and branch links by their delay variables; the two lower vertices are the receivers.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \node[circle,draw] (o) at (0,1.5) {$o$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v) at (2,1.5) {$v$};
  \node[circle,draw] (r1) at (4,2.5) {$r_1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (r2) at (4,0.5) {$r_2$};
  \draw[->] (o) -- node[above] {$X_0$} (v);
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[above] {$X_1$} (r1);
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[below] {$X_2$} (r2);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

Let a rooted tree have root $o$, one shared link $e_0=(o,v)$, and two branch links $e_1=(v,r_1)$ and $e_2=(v,r_2)$ to leaf receivers $r_1,r_2$. Their success probabilities are $p_0,p_1,p_2\in(0,1)$. Compare separate unicast designs to $r_1$ and $r_2$ with a multicast design that sends each probe to both receivers and records both outcomes. For any fixed positive probe counts, the distinct parameter vectors $(1/2,1/2,1/4)$ and $(1/3,3/4,3/8)$ give the same separate-unicast laws but different multicast laws. In fact, the multicast law identifies all three link probabilities for every interior parameter vector.

The diagram labels each edge by its link-success probability; the two lower vertices are the receivers.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \node[circle,draw] (o) at (0,1.5) {$o$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v) at (2,1.5) {$v$};
  \node[circle,draw] (r1) at (4,2.5) {$r_1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (r2) at (4,0.5) {$r_2$};
  \draw[->] (o) -- node[above] {$p_0$} (v);
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[above] {$p_1$} (r1);
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[below] {$p_2$} (r2);
\end{tikzpicture}
```
